# =============================================================================
# gitleaks ignore file. Loaded via `-i ci/gitleaksignore` — gitleaks only
# AUTO-loads an ignore file named `.gitleaksignore` at the --source root
# (cmd/root.go), so keeping it here makes the flag mandatory.
#
# FORMAT: these are GLOBAL (commit-independent) fingerprints —
#   file:rule-id:start-line
# NOT the 4-part `commit:file:rule-id:start-line` form that `gitleaks -v`
# prints. Both are accepted (detect.go `AddFinding` checks the 3-part
# globalFingerprint first, then the commit-scoped one), but the 4-part form is
# unusable here:
#
#   * The scan runs in the `security` matrix leg `secrets`, with
#     `fetch-depth: 0`, so it scans FULL history and a finding is reported under
#     its ORIGINAL commit SHA.
#   * On a pull_request, GitHub checks out an ephemeral `refs/pull/N/merge`
#     commit. Any shallow scan therefore reports the same finding under a
#     DIFFERENT, per-run synthetic SHA ("1 commits scanned"), which no committed
#     fingerprint can ever match.
#
# This used to describe TWO scan contexts, `fast-checks` and
# `security-scan-secrets`. Neither job exists: single-owner consolidation merged
# them into the one `security` matrix leg above, which is why there is a single
# `security[secrets]` signal. The fingerprint-format constraint is unchanged —
# commit-pinned entries are still wrong, because the ephemeral merge-commit SHA
# makes them unmatchable on the pull-request path. Global fingerprints work in
# both cases.
#
# To regenerate: run the scan, take each `Fingerprint:` line, and DROP the
# leading `<commit>:` segment.
#   gitleaks detect --redact --no-banner -v \
#     --config ci/gitleaks.toml -i ci/gitleaksignore --source .
#
# Each entry below was manually triaged (see ci/gitleaks.toml for the pointer):
#
#   NOTE: the identity.py `verify_exp=not allow_expired` false positive used
#   to be triaged here as two line-pinned fingerprints (identity.py:133 and
#   identity.py:159). Line-pinning a suppression to a false positive whose
#   line number moves on every unrelated edit is fragile by construction —
#   it silently re-broke CI once already (the second entry drifted to line
#   161). It is now an exact-substring entry in ci/gitleaks.toml's
#   `[allowlist].regexes`, which matches the finding's own text regardless
#   of which line it lands on. See that file for the full rationale.
#
#   - generic-api-key on infra/terraform/velocityai-alb/README.md:14 — false
#     positive. Matches the prose "Secrets: none created or read by this
#     layer." The internal-ALB layer has since been DELETED, but this entry
#     MUST STAY: the `security` matrix leg `secrets` scans full history (fetch-depth: 0), so the
#     finding is still reported from the commits that predate the removal.
#     Same reason the two entries below survive their deleted files.
#   - generic-api-key on infra/terraform/foundation/README.md:13 — false
#     positive. Matches the prose "Secrets: `app_secret_key` / `db_password`
#     default empty and are auto-generated...".
#   - aws-access-token on backend/evals/minimal/OPUS-AUTOMATIC/FINDINGS.md:167
#     — a real STS temporary session ID pasted into a debugging doc.
#     Confirmed EXPIRED by the doc's own text (the session died ~23 minutes
#     after issue, months before this scan). The file no longer exists on
#     any branch. (Value deliberately not quoted here: naming it re-triggered
#     this very rule on this file.)
#   - jwt on .vscode/settings.json:5 — a real signed JWT committed to a
#     local REST Client config. Decoded `exp` claim is in the past
#     (confirmed expired). The file no longer exists on any branch and
#     `.vscode/` is gitignored going forward.
#
# Rotate-then-allowlist, never allowlist-first: both real (non-false-positive)
# findings above were independently confirmed dead before being listed here.
#
# --- Batch triaged from run 35964176737 (4 findings, all reported from commit
# --- 9efb704d, the "Temp/merge (#10)" squash onto dev). Three were false
# --- positives and are NOT listed here: they were suppressed structurally in
# --- ci/gitleaks.toml instead, per the line-drift warning above. Scanning
# --- locally with those three line-pinned proved the warning empirically — the
# --- SAME logical false positives came straight back from OTHER commits at
# --- different line numbers (model_factory.py at 458 rather than 497,
# --- test_pages_outside_routes.py at 29 rather than 31), plus two more
# --- occurrences of an identical doc string in two separate card trees. A
# --- full-history scan reports a finding once per commit that touches the blob,
# --- so a `file:rule:line` pin can only ever suppress ONE of those commits.
# --- See ci/gitleaks.toml for the regex and path entries that replaced them.
#
#   - jwt on evidence/qa-ps-049/02-validate-attempt1-artifacts-call1.txt:11 —
#     REAL. A signed session JWT captured verbatim into a QA evidence dump as
#     part of a recorded `authorization: Bearer` request header, against
#     http://localhost:3000/. Confirmed DEAD before being listed here, per the
#     rule above: the decoded `exp` claim is 2026-09-23T16:10:19Z against an
#     `iat` of 2026-09-22T16:10:19Z — a 24-hour token that expired before this
#     scan ran. The live file has also been redacted to
#     `<REDACTED-EXPIRED-SESSION-JWT>`, so the value is gone going forward; this
#     entry is required only because the secrets leg scans FULL history
#     (fetch-depth: 0) and the finding is still reported from commit 9efb704d.
#     (Value deliberately not quoted here — naming it would re-trigger this very
#     rule on this very file, exactly as the aws-access-token note above warns.)
#
#     PROCESS NOTE, not a suppression: the underlying defect is that QA evidence
#     capture writes request headers verbatim. Redacting one file does not stop
#     the next capture from committing a live token. Sanitising `authorization`
#     at capture time is the actual fix and is not done here.
